WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.200
So I already did a video on OSINT a&nbsp;
while back. Decent video, solid tools,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:10.160
did its job. But since then I've gone way&nbsp;
deeper into this rabbit hole and learned tricks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:13.440
that genuinely made me sit back in my chair like&nbsp;
there's no way this is legal to know. So tonight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:15.920
I'm dropping everything I've found since then

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:21.040
the stuff nobody else is sharing, the&nbsp;
actual FBI type shi, tools that make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:24.376
Maltego and Shodan look like the&nbsp;
beginner tier they actually are.

00:00:24.376 --> 00:00:24.459
Quick reality check before anything else.&nbsp;
Everything here uses public information.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.459 --> 00:00:24.544
No breaching, no hacking, no unauthorized&nbsp;
access. The reason this matters as much as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.544 --> 00:00:24.634
it does is because almost nobody understands&nbsp;
how much of their life is sitting in public,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.634 --> 00:00:24.721
searchable, cross-referenceable places.&nbsp;
Understanding these tools is how investigators&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.721 --> 00:00:24.800
find people. It's also exactly how you&nbsp;
find out what's findable about yourself.

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:31.040
Let's start with something most people sleep on&nbsp;
completely, Pipl and Spokeo are fine for surface&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:36.880
level stuff but the real professionals reach for&nbsp;
something called IntelTechniques, built by Michael&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:43.200
Bazzell, a former FBI cyber crimes investigator&nbsp;
who now runs the most respected OSINT training&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:48.960
operation in the field. His site has a free search&nbsp;
tool collection that aggregates dozens of lookups&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:56.240
into one place, usernames, emails, phone numbers,&nbsp;
all cross-referenced instantly. This is the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:02.400
methodology actual federal investigators are&nbsp;
trained on, available to you for free, right now.

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:08.160
Now let's talk about something that will genuinely&nbsp;
blow your mind, Epieos. You give it an email&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:13.760
address and it tells you which Google services&nbsp;
that email is tied to, whether there's a profile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:20.160
photo associated with it, sometimes even a real&nbsp;
name pulled straight from Google's own systems.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:26.480
People think their email is anonymous. Epieos will&nbsp;
frequently prove otherwise in about four seconds.

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:32.480
Sherlock is the next one and if you've never used&nbsp;
it, prepare to lose an evening. You give Sherlock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:37.600
a single username and it checks that exact&nbsp;
username against over four hundred websites&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:42.800
simultaneously to tell you everywhere that&nbsp;
name has an account. Someone uses the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:47.920
username on their throwaway Reddit and their&nbsp;
real Instagram? Sherlock just connected them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:51.520
Run it from any terminal, `python3 sherlock.py&nbsp;
username`, and watch the results populate in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:57.600
real time. This is genuinely one of the most&nbsp;
underrated tools in the entire OSINT space.

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:02.720
Then there's Holehe, which does something&nbsp;
similarly unsettling but with emails instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:08.720
of usernames. Feed it an email and it silently&nbsp;
checks that address against over a hundred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:14.400
platforms to see which ones it's registered on,&nbsp;
without sending a single password reset email,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:18.800
without alerting anyone. It works by&nbsp;
exploiting subtle differences in how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:24.800
registration pages respond to existing versus&nbsp;
nonexistent accounts. One email address in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:30.000
and thirty seconds later you have a partial&nbsp;
map of someone's entire digital footprint.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:35.920
Now here's where it gets genuinely federal level.&nbsp;
GrayKey and Cellebrite are the actual tools law&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:41.040
enforcement uses to extract data from seized&nbsp;
phones, but the public-facing equivalent that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:46.880
regular researchers can access is something called&nbsp;
Cellebrite's own published methodology documents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:52.640
and more practically, Hunchly, a browser extension&nbsp;
built specifically for investigators that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:58.560
automatically captures and timestamps every single&nbsp;
page you visit during an investigation, building a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:04.240
fully documented evidence trail as you browse.&nbsp;
Real investigators use this so that everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:07.600
they find is admissible and reconstructable later.&nbsp;
Most OSINT hobbyists have never even heard of it.

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:12.320
For geolocation, forget basic reverse&nbsp;
image search for a second and look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:17.440
something called Overpass Turbo combined&nbsp;
with OpenStreetMap data. Investigators&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:22.800
use this to cross-reference visual landmarks&nbsp;
in a photo, a specific style of streetlight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:28.800
a particular road marking, an unusual building&nbsp;
shape, against actual geographic databases to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:33.200
pin down a location with shocking&nbsp;
precision. This is exactly the kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:38.480
of technique Bellingcat uses to geolocate&nbsp;
conflict footage down to the exact street.

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:42.720
SpiderFoot deserves a mention here too&nbsp;
because it automates almost everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:48.320
I just described into a single scan.&nbsp;
Point it at a domain, an email, or an IP,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:53.680
and it pulls from dozens of data sources&nbsp;
simultaneously, including breach databases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:57.840
building a full relationship map&nbsp;
automatically. It's open source,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:03.200
it runs locally, and most people who've heard&nbsp;
the name still have never actually run it.

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:07.200
And then there's something most&nbsp;
OSINT content completely ignores,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:12.400
court records and public filing databases.&nbsp;
PACER for federal court records in the US,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:17.520
and equivalent public registries in most&nbsp;
other countries, contain an enormous amount&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:23.440
of information that nobody thinks to check.&nbsp;
Lawsuits, property disputes, business filings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:28.960
all public, all searchable, and frequently&nbsp;
containing addresses, employment history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:33.840
and financial details that no social&nbsp;
media platform will ever give you.

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:38.880
None of these tools are illegal. None of them&nbsp;
are hacking. Every single one works because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:45.200
the information was already public, posted, filed,&nbsp;
registered, or leaked into a breach database that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:46.466
anyone can search. The skill in OSINT isn't&nbsp;
accessing secret information. It's knowing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.466 --> 00:04:46.560
that the information was never secret to begin&nbsp;
with, and knowing exactly where to look for it.

